{
  "id": "Compound::DB00451 - Gene::7067",
  "verbalization": "Levothyroxine is implicated in the pathogenesis of multiple skeletal disorders, including Craniosynostosis, Slipped Femoral Capital Epiphysis, and Epiphysiolysis, all of which are linked to Dextrothyroxine. These conditions are mediated through a common pathway involving Dextrothyroxine, which binds to Thra, a key component of the thyroid agent. The evidence suggests that Levothyroxine, through its interaction with Dextrothyroxine, may influence Thra's activity, potentially altering cellular processes associated with bone development and maintenance. The shared ancestry of Levothyroxine and Dextrothyroxine within the Thyroxine family underscores a broader regulatory mechanism where thyroid hormones, in their various forms, interact with Thra to modulate physiological functions. This integrated pathway highlights the role of Dextrothyroxine as a critical mediator in the relationship between Levothyroxine and Thra, emphasizing the importance of thyroid hormone regulation in skeletal health.",
